Q: How to monkey patch Leaflet library? A: <p>Rollup (Vite's build bundler) tree-shakes side-effect-only imports like <code>import &quot;./l.ellipse&quot;</code> in production because it doesn't see <code>L</code> explicitly imported inside <code>l.ellipse.js</code>.</p>
<p>If <code>src/l.ellipse.js</code> is a local file in your project, the cleanest fix is to import Leaflet directly at the top of <code>l.ellipse.js</code>:</p>
<pre><code>import L from &quot;leaflet&quot;;

L.Ellipse = L.Path.extend({ ... });
L.ellipse = function (...) { ... };

export default L;
</code></pre>
<p>This binds the plugin's side effects directly to the <code>leaflet</code> module, ensuring Rollup won't strip or reorder it during the build.</p>
<p>If you can't edit <code>l.ellipse.js</code> directly, you can tell Vite to preserve its side effects via <code>vite.config.ts</code>:</p>
<pre><code>import { defineConfig } from &quot;vite&quot;;
import react from &quot;@vitejs/plugin-react&quot;;

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react()],
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      moduleSideEffects: (id) =&gt; id.includes(&quot;l.ellipse&quot;),
    },
  },
});
</code></pre>
<p>With that config in place, your original import in <code>src/map.tsx</code> will work fine in production:</p>
<pre><code>import * as L from &quot;leaflet&quot;;
import &quot;./l.ellipse&quot;;
</code></pre>

Q: valgrind - Address ---- is 0 bytes after a block of size 8 alloc&#39;d A: <p>I had the same message but it turns out it wasn't an error, i was calculating the padding for the string, something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>    char *next = element_to_string(element-&gt;next);
    int title_padding = 4;
    int element_padding = 14;

    size_t len = strlen(element-&gt;title) + title_padding;
    if (element-&gt;password != NULL) {
        len += strlen(element-&gt;password) + element_padding;
    }
    if (element-&gt;username != NULL) {
        len += strlen(element-&gt;username) + element_padding;
    }
    if (element-&gt;website != NULL) {
        len += strlen(element-&gt;website) + element_padding;
    }
    if (next != NULL) {
        len += strlen(next);
    }

    char *str = calloc(len, sizeof(char));
</code></pre>
<p>In this case the \0 was already added in the title_padding, the only think a needed to do was add the +1 inside calloc instead of adding it directly in title_padding, just changing that remove the error message.</p>
<pre><code>int title_padding = 3;
...
char *str = calloc(len + 1, sizeof(char));
</code></pre>

Q: Instantiate new typed object from domain model A: <p>I had a similar problem. The way I solved it was to set default values to the variables.</p>

<pre><code>export class Reservation {
  public ReservationId: number = null;
  public SeatingId: number = null;
  public EventId: number = null;

  // ...

  constructor() {}
}
</code></pre>
